#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088585} :activity/id #uuid "29565a2e-2dd2-559d-9baf-d688e7b3a204" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048535} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088585 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088920 :tutorial/content "**Euler's formula** allows us to convert between complex exponential functions and complex numbers expressed in polar form. It states that\n\n$$\ne^{\\textrm{i}\\theta} = \\cos\\theta + \\textrm{i}\\sin\\theta.\n$$\n\nWith Euler's formula, we can write any complex number $z$ in three equivalent ways. Letting $r=|z|$ and $\\theta =\\textrm{arg}(z),$ we have the following:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">****</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>Cartesian</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>Polar</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>Exponential</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$z$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$x+\\textrm{i}y$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$r\\left( \\cos\\theta + \\textrm{i}\\sin\\theta\\right)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$re^{\\textrm{i}\\theta}$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nFor instance, if $z=1+\\textrm{i},$ then we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}}=\\sqrt{2}, \\\\ \\theta  & =\\text{arg}(z)=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{1}\\right)=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the complex number $z=1+\\textrm{i}$ can be written in exponential form as\n\n$$\nz=\\sqrt{2}e^{\\textrm{i}\\pi/4}.\n$$\n\nWhen $\\theta=\\pi,$ we have a special case:\n\n$$\ne^{\\textrm{i}\\pi} = -1 \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad e^{\\textrm{i}\\pi} +1=0\n$$\n\nThe equation $e^{\\textrm{i}\\pi} +1=0$ is called **Euler's identity**. It is often described as the most beautiful equation in all of mathematics because it combines five important constants $e, \\textrm{i}, \\pi, 1,$ and $0$ into a single elegant form." :tutorial/id #uuid "1d92b335-3fbb-56fe-86ce-0b0c6a0e5c12" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3445 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "068116bf-6d6b-519b-90aa-1e3c5b3f2d8e" :step/math-academy-id 19479 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088587}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088586 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087734 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087540} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9be02ade-3dd2-5ec3-a532-78d1151dc89f" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050191}] :knowledge-point/level-coverage [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088057} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088287} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244053} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244054} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284724} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284725} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339031} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339033} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348454} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351163} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351167} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351172} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372377} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372384} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373160}] :knowledge-point/title "Writing a Complex Number Shown on an Argand Diagram in Exponential Form"} :step/id #uuid "44134b9f-7dd4-503a-98ec-382788da1087" :step/math-academy-id 19480 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088751}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088587 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087735 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087541} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "01caf021-de3e-5e2a-89ce-ba289b65c0ff" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051044}] :knowledge-point/level-coverage [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088058} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088288} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244052} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284726} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284727} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284728} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284729} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339034} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339035} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339036} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348443} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348446} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348451} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348453} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371541} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373167} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373250}] :knowledge-point/title "Writing a Complex Number in Exponential Form"} :step/id #uuid "a82f4956-1517-5ec2-8edc-d80490783b7c" :step/math-academy-id 19482 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088586}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088751 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088854 :tutorial/content "We can use Euler's formula to construct straightforward proofs of some trigonometric identities.\n\nFor example, let's prove the following:\n\n$$\n\\cos(x+y) = \\cos x\\cos y - \\sin x\\sin y, \\qquad \\sin(x+y) = \\sin x\\cos y + \\cos x\\sin y\n$$\n\nBy the rules of exponents, we have that\n\n$$\ne^{\\textrm i (x+y)} = e^{\\textrm i x} \\cdot e^{\\textrm iy}.\n$$\n\nUsing Euler's formula, this can be written as\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\cos(x+y) + \\textrm i\\sin(x+y)}_{e^{\\textrm i (x+y)} } = \\underbrace{\\left(\\cos x + \\textrm i\\sin x\\right)}_{e^{\\textrm i x}}\\cdot \\underbrace{\\left(\\cos y + \\textrm i\\sin y\\right)}_{e^{\\textrm iy}}.\n$$\n\n \n\nIf we expand the parentheses on the right-hand side and group real and imaginary parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+y)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y) & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+\\text{i}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+{\\text{i}}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+\\text{i}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+\\text{i}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\cos(x+y) + \\textrm i\\sin(x+y) = \\cos x\\cos y - \\sin x\\sin y  + \\textrm i\\left(\\cos x\\sin y + \\sin x\\cos y\\right).\n$$\n\nEquating the real parts of this equation gives\n\n$$\n\\cos(x+y) = \\cos x\\cos y - \\sin x\\sin y\n$$\n\nand equating the imaginary parts gives\n\n$$\n\\sin(x+y) =  \\sin x\\cos y + \\cos x\\sin y\n$$\n\nas required." :tutorial/id #uuid "19745ca9-5f6b-5800-9f92-a3dab79c7372" :tutorial/math-academy-id 13286 :tutorial/title "Proving the Sum Formulas for Sine and Cosine"} :step/id #uuid "6efcd160-d205-5f76-9516-0e9fd7ec7505" :step/math-academy-id 462196}] :activity/title "Euler's Formula" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087960}]}
